@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `usca-interpretation-time`
IMPORT `usca-holidays`
IMPORT `usca-general-provisions`
IMPORT `usca-operation-of-statutes`

-- Questions the first two passes gave no function for: a rule the text states that a caller could not ask.
--
-- Added after the independent test pass (a test author, working from the text alone, listed the scenarios for which the encoding had no function;
-- these are the ones in which the text states a rule).  Nothing here changes an earlier module: each function sits beside the one it extends.
-- The quotation lines are copied by script from the converter-made plain-text rendering of the sections (not the official text) and checked
-- against it by srcrefs.py.  Target: unstable (REFUSE).  Imports: prelude, daydate and the four modules named above.
--
-- How a function here answers a case outside what its section says.  A function whose name asks what THIS SECTION gives, requires or permits answers
-- FALSE for a case outside the section's condition: FALSE means "this section does not give that result", never "the opposite is true".  A function
-- whose name asks what a thing IS, or what the law at large permits, REFUSES outside the text, because no section says no.
-- Nearly every function below is of the first kind and its name begins with the section it reads; the exceptions are named in NOTES.
--
-- What is NOT here, because the text does not state a rule a function could compute: the date of Lunar New Year and of Diwali, the dates in the
-- history notes, the number of divisions or parts of a code, and the days a caller says are judicial holidays (Code Civ. Proc. § 135 is not deposited).

§ `The bodies and offices the sections name`

DECLARE `Public body` IS ONE OF
    `a state office or agency`
    `a state institution`
    `the University of California`
    `the Legislature`
    `a county board of supervisors`
    `a county agency`
    `the legislative body of a city`
    `the legislative body of a district`

§ `Gov. Code § 6701(c) and (d): March 31 observed elsewhere, and a county's alternate day`

-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:36 | (c) If March 31 falls upon a Tuesday, Wednesday, or Thursday, the Legislature may observe the holiday on one of the following:
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:37 | (1) The preceding Friday.
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:38 | (2) The preceding Monday.
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:39 | (3) The following Friday.
@ref Gov. Code § 6701(c) (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:36-39)
-- When March 31 falls on a Tuesday, Wednesday or Thursday, the three days on which "the Legislature may observe the holiday": the preceding Friday,
-- the preceding Monday and the following Friday.  In any other year the list is empty (F-6701c in NOTES: whether March 31 itself then stops being a holiday).
GIVEN y IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A LIST OF DATE
`the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` y MEANS
    IF weekday EQUALS 2 OR weekday EQUALS 3 OR weekday EQUALS 4
    THEN LIST (march31 MINUS ((weekday PLUS 2) MODULO 7)), (march31 MINUS (weekday MINUS 1)), (march31 PLUS (5 MINUS weekday))
    ELSE EMPTY
    WHERE
        march31 MEANS YMD y 3 31
        weekday MEANS `Weekday of` march31

@ref Gov. Code § 6701(c) (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:36-39)
GIVEN d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6701(c) lets the Legislature observe March 31 on the day` d MEANS
    elem d (`the days on which the Legislature may observe March 31 of the year` (DATE_YEAR d))

-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:40 | (d) If
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:41 | any holiday designated in Section 6700 falls on a Saturday, the board of supervisors of any county may by ordinance or resolution provide that an alternate day shall be a holiday for the employees of the county, except those employees of the county working as court attachés.
@ref Gov. Code § 6701(d) (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:40-41)
-- The power belongs to "the board of supervisors of any county", and arises when "any holiday designated in Section 6700 falls on a Saturday".
GIVEN body IS A `Public body`
      supplied IS A `Days supplied by the caller`
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6701(d) lets the body provide an alternate day for a holiday falling on` body supplied d MEANS
    body EQUALS `a county board of supervisors`
    AND `is a Saturday` d
    AND `a holiday under Gov. Code § 6700 and § 6701 for the state` supplied d

§ `Gov. Code § 6703 to § 6707: closing, Saturdays, special holidays and the next business day`

-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:45 | 6703. (a) Public offices of the state, state institutions, and the University of California shall be closed on Admission Day.
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:46 | (b) Public offices of the state and state institutions, including, but not limited to, all state agencies and the Legislature, shall be closed on November 11, Veterans Day. If, in a given year, November 11 falls on a Saturday,
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:47 | then public offices of the state and state institutions, including the Legislature, shall be closed on Friday, November 10. If, in a given year, November 11 falls on a Sunday, then public offices of the state and state institutions, including the Legislature, shall be closed on Monday, November 12. It is the intent of the Legislature that the University of California also be closed on the Veterans Day holiday.
@ref Gov. Code § 6703 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:45-47)
-- (a): "Public offices of the state, state institutions, and the University of California shall be closed on Admission Day."  (b): the offices of the
-- state and state institutions "including, but not limited to, all state agencies and the Legislature" are closed on Veterans Day, and "It is the intent
-- of the Legislature that the University of California also be closed" then: an intent, not a requirement, so FALSE.  Whether (a) reaches the Legislature
-- is not said: (a) does not name it and (b) names it among the public offices of the state (F-6703leg in NOTES), so Admission Day for the Legislature is REFUSED.
GIVEN body IS A `Public body`
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6703 requires the body to be closed on the day` body d MEANS
    CONSIDER body
    WHEN `a state office or agency` THEN `Gov. Code § 6703 requires public offices of the state to be closed on the day` d
    WHEN `a state institution` THEN `Gov. Code § 6703 requires public offices of the state to be closed on the day` d
    WHEN `the University of California` THEN `the date is the given day of the given month` d 9 9
    WHEN `the Legislature` THEN
        IF `the date is the given day of the given month` d 9 9
        THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 6703(a) names the public offices of the state, state institutions and the University of California for Admission Day and does not say whether the Legislature is among them; § 6703(b) names the Legislature for Veterans Day only"
        ELSE `Gov. Code § 6703 requires public offices of the state to be closed on the day` d
    WHEN `a county board of supervisors` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a county agency` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the legislative body of a city` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `the legislative body of a district` THEN FALSE

-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:49 | 6704. The legislative body of any city or district may, by ordinance or resolution, provide that every Saturday is a holiday as respects the transaction of business in the public offices of such cities or districts except that provision shall be made for the continuance of essential public services such as police and fire protection.
@ref Gov. Code § 6704 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:49)
-- "The legislative body of any city or district may" make every Saturday a holiday for its public offices.  A county is not named (F-6704county in NOTES).
GIVEN body IS A `Public body`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6704 gives the body the power to make every Saturday a holiday for its public offices` body MEANS
    body EQUALS `the legislative body of a city` OR body EQUALS `the legislative body of a district`

@ref Gov. Code § 6704 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:49)
-- "except that provision shall be made for the continuance of essential public services such as police and fire protection."
GIVEN `the legislative body has made every Saturday a holiday for its public offices` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6704 requires provision for the continuance of essential public services such as police and fire protection` `the legislative body has made every Saturday a holiday for its public offices` MEANS
    `the legislative body has made every Saturday a holiday for its public offices`

-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:51 | 6705. A special or limited holiday is a holiday applying only to a special class or classes of business, or a special class or classes of persons, and not appointed to be generally observed throughout the State by all classes of business and all classes of persons.
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:52 | On any special or limited holiday appointed by the President or Governor, all courts and public offices of the State, any political subdivision, or any city, shall be open and function in their normal and usual manner. All other public functions shall be performed as on days which are not holidays, and all contracts shall be performed and business transacted as usual, except only
-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:53 | as to or by the particular class of business or persons expressly limited or restricted by the provisions of the proclamation appointing or declaring such special or limited holiday.
@ref Gov. Code § 6705 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:51-53)
-- A special or limited holiday applies "only to a special class or classes of business, or a special class or classes of persons, and not" to all of them.
GIVEN `it applies only to a special class or classes of business, or of persons` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is appointed to be generally observed throughout the State by all classes of business and persons` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a holiday is a special or limited holiday under Gov. Code § 6705` `it applies only to a special class or classes of business, or of persons` `it is appointed to be generally observed throughout the State by all classes of business and persons` MEANS
    `it applies only to a special class or classes of business, or of persons` AND NOT `it is appointed to be generally observed throughout the State by all classes of business and persons`

@ref Gov. Code § 6705 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:51-53)
-- "all contracts shall be performed and business transacted as usual, except only as to or by the particular class of business or persons expressly
-- limited or restricted by the provisions of the proclamation".
GIVEN `the party is within the class expressly limited or restricted by the proclamation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6705 has a contract performed and business transacted as usual by the party on a special or limited holiday` `the party is within the class expressly limited or restricted by the proclamation` MEANS
    NOT `the party is within the class expressly limited or restricted by the proclamation`

DECLARE `Source of an appointed day` IS ONE OF
    `a law`
    `a contract`
    `a court order`

-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:55 | 6706. Whenever any act of a secular nature, other than a work of necessity or mercy, is appointed by law or contract to be performed upon a particular day which falls upon a holiday, such act may be performed upon the next business day with the same effect as if it had been performed upon the day appointed.
@ref Gov. Code § 6706 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:55)
-- "appointed by law or contract".  A court order is neither named nor excluded.
GIVEN source IS A `Source of an appointed day`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6706 applies to an act appointed to be performed on a particular day by` source MEANS
    CONSIDER source
    WHEN `a law` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a contract` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a court order` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 6706 speaks of a day appointed by law or contract; it does not say whether a day fixed by a court order is one"

@ref Gov. Code § 6706 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:55)
-- "such act may be performed upon the next business day": a permission (Gov. Code § 14: "may" is permissive), not a duty.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6706 requires the act to be performed on the next business day` MEANS FALSE

-- src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:57 | 6707. When the last day for filing any instrument or other document with a state agency falls upon a Saturday or holiday, such act may be performed upon the next business day with the same effect as if it had been performed upon the day appointed.
@ref Gov. Code § 6707 (src:GOV-ss-6700-6736:57)
-- "with a state agency".  A county agency is not named.
GIVEN body IS A `Public body`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6707 gives the next business day for a filing with` body MEANS
    body EQUALS `a state office or agency`

§ `Gov. Code § 6801, § 6802, § 6804, § 6808(b)`

-- src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:10 | 6801. Time is computed according to the Gregorian or new style; and January 1st, in every year, after 1752 is reckoned as the first day of the year.
@ref Gov. Code § 6801 (src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:10)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6801 computes time according to the Gregorian calendar` MEANS TRUE

-- src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:12 | 6802. The years 1900, 2100, 2200, 2300, or any other future hundredth year, of which the year 2000 is the first, except only every fourth hundredth year, are not leap years, but common years of 365 days. The years 2000, 2400, 2800, and every other fourth hundredth year after 2000 and every fourth year, except as provided in this section, which, by usage in this State is considered a leap year, is a leap year consisting of 366 days.
@ref Gov. Code § 6802 (src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:12)
GIVEN year IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A NUMBER
`days in the year under Gov. Code § 6802` year MEANS
    IF `is a leap year under section 6802` year THEN 366 ELSE 365

-- src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:16 | 6804. “Month” means a calendar month, unless otherwise expressed.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:64 | (4) The word “month” means a calendar month, unless otherwise expressed.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:64 | (4) “Month” means a calendar month, unless otherwise expressed.
@ref Gov. Code § 6804, Civ. Code § 14(b)(4), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b)(4) (src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:16, src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:64, src:CCP-ss-1-34:64)
-- "'Month' means a calendar month, unless otherwise expressed."  What a month then is, when it is otherwise expressed, is not said (a lunar month, a 30-day
-- month), so this function says only whether the section makes the word mean a calendar month.
GIVEN `a different meaning is otherwise expressed` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 6804 gives the word month the meaning of a calendar month` `a different meaning is otherwise expressed` MEANS
    NOT `a different meaning is otherwise expressed`

-- src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:25 | (b) The standard time within the state shall advance by one hour during the daylight saving time period commencing at 2 a.m. on the second Sunday of March of each year and ending at 2 a.m. on the first Sunday of November of each year.
@ref Gov. Code § 6808(b) (src:GOV-ss-6800-6808:25)
-- "commencing at 2 a.m. on the second Sunday of March ... and ending at 2 a.m. on the first Sunday of November": 2 a.m. is 7,200 seconds after midnight.
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the clock reading, in seconds after midnight, at which daylight saving time begins and ends under Gov. Code § 6808(b)` MEANS 7200

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(b), § 12b, § 13a, § 13b and § 33`

DECLARE `Source of a period of time` IS ONE OF
    `Section 659`
    `Section 659a`
    `Section 921`
    `another provision of the Code of Civil Procedure`
    `a provision of another code or statute`
    `an ordinance`
    `a rule`
    `a regulation`
    `a contract`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:37 | (b) This section
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:38 | applies to Sections 659, 659a, and 921, and to all other provisions of law providing or requiring an act to be performed on a particular day or within a specified period of time, whether expressed in this or any other code or statute, ordinance, rule, or regulation.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(b) (src:CCP-ss-1-34:37-38)
-- "applies to Sections 659, 659a, and 921, and to all other provisions of law providing or requiring an act to be performed on a particular day or within
-- a specified period of time, whether expressed in this or any other code or statute, ordinance, rule, or regulation."  A contract is not a provision of law
-- by that text, and the text does not say it is not.
GIVEN source IS A `Source of a period of time`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Code Civ. Proc. § 12a applies to a period fixed by` source MEANS
    CONSIDER source
    WHEN `Section 659` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Section 659a` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Section 921` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `another provision of the Code of Civil Procedure` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a provision of another code or statute` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an ordinance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a rule` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a regulation` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a contract` THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(b) applies to provisions of law in a code, statute, ordinance, rule or regulation; it does not say whether a period fixed by a contract is one"

DECLARE `Office` IS ONE OF
    `a city office`
    `a county office`
    `a state office`
    `another public office`
    `a branch office`
    `a private office`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:40 | 12b. If any city, county, state, or public office, other than a branch office, is closed for the whole of any day, insofar as the business of that office is concerned, that day shall be considered as a holiday for the purposes of computing time under Sections 12 and 12a.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12b (src:CCP-ss-1-34:40)
-- "If any city, county, state, or public office, other than a branch office, is closed for the whole of any day ... that day shall be considered as a
-- holiday".  A private office is not a public office.
GIVEN office IS AN `Office`
      `the office is closed for the whole of the day` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Code Civ. Proc. § 12b makes the day a holiday for computing time when the office is closed for the whole of it` office `the office is closed for the whole of the day` MEANS
    CONSIDER office
    WHEN `a city office` THEN `the office is closed for the whole of the day`
    WHEN `a county office` THEN `the office is closed for the whole of the day`
    WHEN `a state office` THEN `the office is closed for the whole of the day`
    WHEN `another public office` THEN `the office is closed for the whole of the day`
    WHEN `a branch office` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a private office` THEN FALSE

DECLARE `Provision that counts time` IS ONE OF
    `Code Civ. Proc. § 12`
    `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a`
    `Gov. Code § 6800`

@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12b (src:CCP-ss-1-34:40)
-- "for the purposes of computing time under Sections 12 and 12a": Gov. Code § 6800 says the same as § 12 but is not named.
GIVEN provision IS A `Provision that counts time`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Code Civ. Proc. § 12b applies to the computation of time under` provision MEANS
    CONSIDER provision
    WHEN `Code Civ. Proc. § 12` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Code Civ. Proc. § 12a` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `Gov. Code § 6800` THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12b names Sections 12 and 12a of the Code of Civil Procedure; it does not say whether a day it makes a holiday counts under Gov. Code § 6800, which says the same as § 12"

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:48 | 13a. Any act required by law to be performed on a particular day or within a specified period of time may be performed (but is not hereby required to be performed) on a special holiday as that term is used in Section 6705 of the Government Code, with like effect as if performed on a day which is not a holiday.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:50 | 13b. Any act required by law to be performed on a particular day or within a specified period may be performed (but is not hereby required to be performed) on a Saturday, with like effect as if performed on a day which is not a holiday.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 13a, § 13b (src:CCP-ss-1-34:48, src:CCP-ss-1-34:50)
-- "may be performed (but is not hereby required to be performed)": neither section requires the act on a special holiday or on a Saturday, and
-- neither says anything about a Sunday or another holiday (the same answer for every kind).
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of day for performing an act`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Code Civ. Proc. § 13a or § 13b requires an act to be performed on` kind MEANS FALSE

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:126 | 33. A prosecuting attorney, in his or her discretion, may assist in the civil resolution of a violation of an offense described in Title 13 (commencing with Section 450) of Part 1 of the Penal Code in lieu of filing a criminal complaint.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 33 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:126)
-- "A prosecuting attorney, in his or her discretion, may assist": a permission.
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Code Civ. Proc. § 33 requires a prosecuting attorney to assist in the civil resolution of a violation` MEANS FALSE

§ `Gov. Code § 14, § 12.5, § 24, § 16.5(a)(5)`

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:57 | 14. “Shall” is mandatory and “may” is permissive.
@ref Gov. Code § 14 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:57)
-- "'Shall' is mandatory and 'may' is permissive."  The section defines two words; it says nothing of "must" or of any other word.
GIVEN word IS A STRING
GIVETH A `Force of a word`
`the force of the word as written in Gov. Code § 14` word MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF word EQUALS "shall" THEN `the force of the word in the Government Code` shall
        IF word EQUALS "may" THEN `the force of the word in the Government Code` may
        OTHERWISE REFUSE "Gov. Code § 14 says that shall is mandatory and may is permissive; it does not say what any other word, such as must, is"

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:53 | 12.5. The Legislature hereby declares its intent that the terms “man” or “men” where appropriate shall be deemed “person” or “persons” and any references to the terms “man” or “men” in sections of this code be changed to “person” or “persons” when such code sections are being amended for any purpose. This act is declaratory and not amendatory of existing law.
-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:90 | 24. The Legislature hereby declares its intent that the term “workmen’s compensation” shall hereafter also be known as “workers’ compensation.” In furtherance of this policy it is the desire of the Legislature that references to the term “workmen’s compensation” in this code be changed to “workers’ compensation” when such code sections are being amended for any purpose. This act is declaratory and not amendatory of existing law.
@ref Gov. Code § 12.5, § 24 (src:GOV-ss-1-26:53, src:GOV-ss-1-26:90)
-- "This act is declaratory and not amendatory of existing law."
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the declaration of Gov. Code § 12.5 or § 24 is amendatory of existing law` MEANS FALSE

-- src:GOV-ss-1-26:68 | (5) It conforms to regulations adopted by the Secretary of State. Initial regulations shall be adopted no later than January 1, 1997. In developing these regulations, the secretary shall seek the advice of public and private entities, including, but not limited to, the Department of Information Technology, the California Environmental Protection Agency, and the Department of General Services. Before the secretary adopts the regulations, he or she shall hold at least one public hearing to receive comments.
@ref Gov. Code § 16.5(a)(5) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:68)
-- "Initial regulations shall be adopted no later than January 1, 1997."
GIVETH A DATE
`the latest date for the Secretary of State's initial regulations under Gov. Code § 16.5(a)(5)` MEANS YMD 1997 1 1

@ref Gov. Code § 16.5(a)(5) (src:GOV-ss-1-26:68)
-- "he or she shall hold at least one public hearing to receive comments."
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the fewest public hearings the Secretary of State must hold before adopting the regulations under Gov. Code § 16.5(a)(5)` MEANS 1

§ `Gov. Code § 9602, § 9604, § 9605(b), § 9607(b), § 9608, § 9610, § 9611`

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:11 | 9602. Every concurrent and joint resolution takes effect upon the filing of it with the Secretary of State.
@ref Gov. Code § 9602 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:11)
-- "takes effect upon the filing of it with the Secretary of State": a resolution not yet filed is not in effect.
GIVEN m IS A Moment
      filed IS A MAYBE Moment
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a concurrent or joint resolution is in effect at the moment` m `given that it was filed with the Secretary of State at` filed MEANS
    CONSIDER filed
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST at THEN `the moment` m `is not before the moment` (`the moment a concurrent or joint resolution takes effect, having been filed with the Secretary of State at` at)

DECLARE `Source of a reference` IS ONE OF
    `a statute of the State`
    `a charter`
    `an ordinance`
    `a contract`
    `a regulation`

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:15 | 9604. When the provisions of one statute are carried into another statute under circumstances in which they are required to be construed as restatements and continuations and not as new enactments, any reference made by any statute, charter or ordinance to such provisions shall, unless a contrary intent appears, be deemed a reference to the restatements and continuations.
@ref Gov. Code § 9604 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:15)
-- "any reference made by any statute, charter or ordinance": a contract and a regulation are not named.
GIVEN source IS A `Source of a reference`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9604 applies to a reference made by` source MEANS
    CONSIDER source
    WHEN `a statute of the State` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a charter` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `an ordinance` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a contract` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9604 speaks of a reference made by a statute, charter or ordinance; it does not say whether a reference in a contract is one"
    WHEN `a regulation` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9604 speaks of a reference made by a statute, charter or ordinance; it does not say whether a reference in a regulation is one"

DECLARE `Presumption of Gov. Code § 9605(b)` IS ONE OF
    `the presumption of paragraph (1)`
    `the presumption of paragraph (2)`

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:20 | (b) When the same section or part of a statute is amended by two or more statutes enacted at the same session:
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:21 | (1) In the absence of any express provision to the contrary in the statute that is enacted last, it shall be conclusively presumed that the statute that is enacted last is intended to prevail over statutes that are enacted earlier at the same session.
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:22 | (2) In the absence of any express provision to the contrary in the statute with a higher chapter number, it shall be presumed that the statute with a higher chapter number is intended
-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:23 | by the Legislature to prevail over a statute that is enacted at the same session with a lower chapter number. For the purposes of this paragraph, every statute enacted in the even-numbered year of a two-year regular session of the Legislature is deemed to bear a higher chapter number than any statute enacted in the odd-numbered year of that session.
@ref Gov. Code § 9605(b) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:20-23)
-- (1): "it shall be conclusively presumed"; (2): "it shall be presumed".  Whether a conclusive presumption therefore outranks a presumption is F-9605b in NOTES.
GIVEN presumption IS A `Presumption of Gov. Code § 9605(b)`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9605(b) makes the presumption conclusive` presumption MEANS
    presumption EQUALS `the presumption of paragraph (1)`

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:28 | (b) If a later enacted statute that deletes or extends the date of termination or repeal of a previously enacted law is chaptered before such date of termination or repeal, the terminated or repealed law is revived when the later enacted statute becomes operative.
@ref Gov. Code § 9607(b) (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:28)
-- Only "a later enacted statute that deletes or extends the date of termination or repeal" revives the law.
GIVEN `the later statute deletes or extends the date of termination or repeal` IS A BOOLEAN
      chaptered IS A DATE
      `the stated date of termination or repeal` IS A DATE
      `the later statute becomes operative on` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9607(b) revives the terminated or repealed law` `the later statute deletes or extends the date of termination or repeal` chaptered `the stated date of termination or repeal` `the later statute becomes operative on` MEANS
    CONSIDER `the day a terminated or repealed law is revived by a later statute chaptered on` chaptered `the stated date of termination or repeal` `the later statute becomes operative on`
    WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
    WHEN JUST revivedOn THEN `the later statute deletes or extends the date of termination or repeal`

DECLARE `Kind of law that ends` IS ONE OF
    `a law creating a criminal offense`
    `a law creating civil liability`

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:30 | 9608. The termination or suspension (by whatsoever means effected) of any law creating a criminal offense does not constitute a bar to the indictment or information and punishment of an act already committed in violation of the law so terminated or suspended, unless the intention to bar such indictment or information and punishment is expressly declared by an applicable provision of law.
@ref Gov. Code § 9608 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:30)
-- "any law creating a criminal offense": a law creating civil liability is not named.
GIVEN kind IS A `Kind of law that ends`
      `a provision of law expressly declares the intention to bar` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9608 leaves an act already committed open to proceedings and punishment after the law ends` kind `a provision of law expressly declares the intention to bar` MEANS
    CONSIDER kind
    WHEN `a law creating a criminal offense` THEN NOT `a provision of law expressly declares the intention to bar`
    WHEN `a law creating civil liability` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9608 speaks of a law creating a criminal offense; it does not say what follows when a law creating civil liability ends"

DECLARE `Holder of a salaried position` IS ONE OF
    `a State officer or employee`
    `a county officer or employee`
    `a city officer or employee`

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:34 | 9610. The fixing or authorizing the fixing of the salary of a State officer or employee by statute is not intended to and does not constitute an appropriation of money for the payment of the salary. The salary shall be paid only in the event that moneys are made available therefor by another provision of law.
@ref Gov. Code § 9610 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:34)
-- "the salary of a State officer or employee": no other holder is named.
GIVEN holder IS A `Holder of a salaried position`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9610 makes the salary fixed by statute payable only if moneys are made available by another provision of law` holder MEANS
    CONSIDER holder
    WHEN `a State officer or employee` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a county officer or employee` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9610 speaks of the salary of a State officer or employee; it does not say what holds for a county officer or employee"
    WHEN `a city officer or employee` THEN REFUSE "Gov. Code § 9610 speaks of the salary of a State officer or employee; it does not say what holds for a city officer or employee"

-- src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:36 | 9611. Notwithstanding any other provision of this chapter, whenever a provision of law is temporarily suspended, or is expressly or impliedly modified or repealed by a provision which is declared to be effective for only a limited period, the original provisions are not to be deemed repealed, but upon the expiration of the time of the temporary suspension or the effectiveness of the inconsistent provision, the original provision shall have the same force and effect as if the temporary provision had not been enacted.
@ref Gov. Code § 9611 (src:GOV-ss-9600-9612:36)
-- "temporarily suspended, or is expressly or impliedly modified or repealed by a provision which is declared to be effective for only a limited period".
GIVEN `a provision of law is temporarily suspended` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the modifying or repealing provision is declared to be effective for only a limited period` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Gov. Code § 9611 keeps the original provision from being deemed repealed` `a provision of law is temporarily suspended` `the modifying or repealing provision is declared to be effective for only a limited period` MEANS
    `a provision of law is temporarily suspended` OR `the modifying or repealing provision is declared to be effective for only a limited period`

§ `Civ. Code § 14(b), § 17, § 20, § 22.2, § 23, § 23.6; Code Civ. Proc. § 9, § 17(b)`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:59 | (b) The following words have in this code the signification attached to them in this section, unless otherwise apparent from the context:
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:59 | (b) As used in this code, the following words have the following meanings, unless otherwise apparent
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:60 | from the context:
@ref Civ. Code § 14(b), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:59, src:CCP-ss-1-34:59-60)
-- "the following words have in this code the signification attached to them in this section, unless otherwise apparent from the context".
GIVEN `it is otherwise apparent from the context` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Civ. Code § 14(b) and Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) give the word the signification they state` `it is otherwise apparent from the context` MEANS
    NOT `it is otherwise apparent from the context`

@ref Civ. Code § 14(b), Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:59, src:CCP-ss-1-34:59-60)
GIVEN `it is otherwise apparent from the context` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a signification stated in Civ. Code § 14(b) or Code Civ. Proc. § 17(b) is displaced` `it is otherwise apparent from the context` MEANS
    `it is otherwise apparent from the context`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:69 | 17. Wherever any notice or other communication is required by this code to be mailed by registered mail, the mailing of such notice or other communication by certified mail shall be deemed to be a sufficient compliance with the requirements of law.
@ref Civ. Code § 17 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:69)
-- "required ... to be mailed by registered mail ... certified mail shall be deemed ... sufficient": one way only.
GIVEN method IS A `Method of mailing`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a requirement of the Civil Code that a notice be mailed by certified mail is met by` method MEANS
    CONSIDER method
    WHEN `certified mail` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `registered mail` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 17 says that certified mail is a sufficient compliance where registered mail is required; it does not say that registered mail is sufficient where certified mail is required"
    WHEN `another method of mailing` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 17 says that certified mail is a sufficient compliance where registered mail is required; it says nothing of a requirement of certified mail"

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:77 | 20. No statute, law, or rule is continued in force because it is consistent with the provisions of this Code on the same subject; but in all cases provided for by this Code, all statutes, laws, and rules heretofore in force in this State, whether consistent or not with the provisions of this Code, unless expressly continued in force by it, are repealed or abrogated.
-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:78 | This repeal or abrogation does not revive any former law heretofore repealed, nor does it affect any right already existing or accrued, or any action or proceeding already taken, except as in this Code provided.
@ref Civ. Code § 20 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:77-78)
-- Civ. Code § 20 has no sentence about a private statute (Code Civ. Proc. § 18 has one, and `the repeal by the Code of Civil Procedure affects a private statute` encodes it).
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the repeal by the Civil Code affects a private statute` MEANS
    REFUSE "Civ. Code § 20 says nothing of a private statute; Code Civ. Proc. § 18 does, and Civ. Code § 20 does not say that it is to be read the same way"

DECLARE `Law of England` IS ONE OF
    `the common law of England`
    `a statute of England`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:88 | 22.2. The common law of England, so far as it is not repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State, is the rule of decision in all the courts of this State.
@ref Civ. Code § 22.2 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:88)
GIVEN law IS A `Law of England`
      `the rule is repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Civ. Code § 22.2 makes the law of England the rule of decision in the courts of this State` law `the rule is repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State` MEANS
    CONSIDER law
    WHEN `the common law of England` THEN `the common law of England is the rule of decision` `the rule is repugnant to or inconsistent with the Constitution of the United States, or the Constitution or laws of this State`
    WHEN `a statute of England` THEN REFUSE "Civ. Code § 22.2 speaks of the common law of England; it does not say whether an English statute is the rule of decision"

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:102 | 23.6. If conflicting provisions are found in different sections of the same chapter or article, the provisions of the sections last in numerical order shall prevail, unless such construction is inconsistent with the meaning of the chapter or article.
@ref Civ. Code § 23.6 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:102)
-- "If conflicting provisions are found in different sections of the same chapter or article": for sections of different chapters, § 23.4 (chapters of one
-- title) or § 23.3 (titles) speaks instead, and this section gives no rule.
GIVEN `the sections are in the same chapter or article` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Civ. Code § 23.6 makes the section last in numerical order prevail` `the sections are in the same chapter or article` MEANS
    `the sections are in the same chapter or article`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:28 | 9. When a limitation or period of time prescribed in any existing statute for acquiring a right or barring a remedy, or for any other purpose, has begun to run before this code goes into effect, and the same or any limitation is prescribed in this code, the time that has already run shall be deemed part of the time prescribed as such limitation by this code.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 9 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:28)
-- The time already run counts "when a limitation or period of time ... has begun to run before this code goes into effect, and the same or any limitation is
-- prescribed in this code".  Where the code prescribes none, the section gives the time already run no place.
GIVEN `the limitation had begun to run before the code goes into effect` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the code prescribes the same or any limitation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Code Civ. Proc. § 9 deems the time already run part of the time the code prescribes` `the limitation had begun to run before the code goes into effect` `the code prescribes the same or any limitation` MEANS
    `the limitation had begun to run before the code goes into effect` AND `the code prescribes the same or any limitation`

-- src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:16 | 3. No part of it is retroactive, unless expressly so declared.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:16 | 3. No part of it is retroactive, unless expressly so declared.
@ref Civ. Code § 3, Code Civ. Proc. § 3 (src:CIV-ss-1-23.6:16, src:CCP-ss-1-34:16)
-- The Civil Code and the Code of Civil Procedure each say "No part of it is retroactive, unless expressly so declared."  The deposited sections of the
-- Government Code (§§ 1-26) have no such sentence (§ 4 speaks of actions begun and rights accrued before the code took effect).
GIVEN code IS A `Kind of code`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the code says that no part of it is retroactive unless expressly so declared` code MEANS
    CONSIDER code
    WHEN `the Civil Code` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Code of Civil Procedure` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the Government Code` THEN REFUSE "the deposited sections of the Government Code have no sentence saying that no part of it is retroactive unless expressly so declared; Gov. Code § 4 speaks of actions begun and rights accrued before the code took effect"

§ `Code Civ. Proc. § 10, § 12, § 12a: the Section 135 days, stated or left out`

-- Code Civ. Proc. § 135 is not in the deposited text, so the judicial holidays it specifies are an input of `Days supplied by the caller`.  A caller who supplies no
-- `Days supplied by the caller` list gets FALSE for 25 December under Code Civ. Proc. §§ 10, 12 and 12a, with no diagnostic: the functions of `usca-holidays` cannot tell
-- an empty list from an omitted one (a SILENT failure, NOTES "Reading a result").  The functions below take the Section 135 days as a `MAYBE`: `NOTHING` means the caller
-- has not said, and they REFUSE; `JUST EMPTY` means the caller says there are none, and they answer from the rest of the record.  The other lists of the record (Lunar
-- New Year, Diwali, Good Friday, Thanksgiving, closure days, bank closings) have the same silent behaviour and no loud variant.
GIVEN supplied IS A `Days supplied by the caller`
      listed IS A LIST OF DATE
GIVETH A `Days supplied by the caller`
`the days supplied, with the Section 135 days stated as` supplied listed MEANS
    `Days supplied by the caller` WITH
        `Lunar New Year days` IS supplied's `Lunar New Year days`
        `Diwali days` IS supplied's `Diwali days`
        `days appointed by the President or Governor for a public fast, thanksgiving or holiday` IS supplied's `days appointed by the President or Governor for a public fast, thanksgiving or holiday`
        `Thursdays appointed as Thanksgiving Day` IS supplied's `Thursdays appointed as Thanksgiving Day`
        `Good Friday days` IS supplied's `Good Friday days`
        `days on which March 31 is observed instead` IS supplied's `days on which March 31 is observed instead`
        `March 31 itself stays a holiday in a year in which it is observed on another day` IS supplied's `March 31 itself stays a holiday in a year in which it is observed on another day`
        `judicial holidays specified in Code Civ. Proc. section 135` IS listed
        `days a public office is closed for the whole day` IS supplied's `days a public office is closed for the whole day`
        `bank closings under the Bank Extraordinary Situation Closing Act` IS supplied's `bank closings under the Bank Extraordinary Situation Closing Act`

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:30 | 10. Holidays within the meaning of this code are every Sunday and any other days that are specified or provided for as judicial holidays in Section 135.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 10 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:30)
GIVEN section135 IS A MAYBE (LIST OF DATE)
      supplied IS A `Days supplied by the caller`
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a holiday under Code Civ. Proc. § 10, the Section 135 days being` section135 supplied d MEANS
    CONSIDER section135
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 10 makes the judicial holidays specified in Section 135 holidays, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
    WHEN JUST listed THEN `a holiday under Code Civ. Proc. § 10` (`the days supplied, with the Section 135 days stated as` supplied listed) d

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:34 | 12. The time in which any act provided by law is to be done is computed by excluding the first day, and including the last, unless the last day is a holiday, and then it is also excluded.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:34)
GIVEN section135 IS A MAYBE (LIST OF DATE)
      supplied IS A `Days supplied by the caller`
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a holiday for computing time under Code Civ. Proc. § 12, the Section 135 days being` section135 supplied d MEANS
    CONSIDER section135
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12 counts the holidays of Code Civ. Proc. § 10, which include the judicial holidays specified in Section 135, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
    WHEN JUST listed THEN `a holiday for computing time under Code Civ. Proc. § 12` (`the days supplied, with the Section 135 days stated as` supplied listed) d

-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:36 | 12a. (a) If the last day for the performance of any act provided or required by law to be performed within a specified period of time is a holiday, then that period is hereby extended to and including the next day that is not a holiday. For purposes of this section, “holiday” means all day on Saturdays, all holidays specified in Section 135 and, to the extent provided in Section 12b, all days that by terms of Section 12b are required to be considered as holidays.
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:37 | (b) This section
-- src:CCP-ss-1-34:38 | applies to Sections 659, 659a, and 921, and to all other provisions of law providing or requiring an act to be performed on a particular day or within a specified period of time, whether expressed in this or any other code or statute, ordinance, rule, or regulation.
@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) (src:CCP-ss-1-34:36-38)
GIVEN section135 IS A MAYBE (LIST OF DATE)
      supplied IS A `Days supplied by the caller`
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), read literally, the Section 135 days being` section135 supplied d MEANS
    CONSIDER section135
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) makes the holidays specified in Section 135 holidays for the extension, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
    WHEN JUST listed THEN `a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), read literally` (`the days supplied, with the Section 135 days stated as` supplied listed) d

@ref Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), § 10 (src:CCP-ss-1-34:36-38)
GIVEN section135 IS A MAYBE (LIST OF DATE)
      supplied IS A `Days supplied by the caller`
      d IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), taking in the Sundays of § 10, the Section 135 days being` section135 supplied d MEANS
    CONSIDER section135
    WHEN NOTHING THEN REFUSE "Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a) makes the holidays specified in Section 135 holidays for the extension, and Section 135 is not in the deposited text; the caller has not said which days it specifies"
    WHEN JUST listed THEN `a holiday for the extension of Code Civ. Proc. § 12a(a), taking in the Sundays of § 10` (`the days supplied, with the Section 135 days stated as` supplied listed) d
